notes[1]{created_at,author,text}:
  "2026-08-10T16:12:50.718Z","harness:claude-code","Reference instance recorded 2026-08-10, and it is the cleanest reproduction of this item so far because the fix and the block happened on the same day.\n\nThe observability issue PM-CLI-2X, titled as an ENOTDIR fault opening a path, was the root cause fixed by the tracker-root guard reachability work earlier that day: an optional read of the context intents file tolerated only ENOENT and threw a raw ENOTDIR before the typed guard could classify it. The product fix shipped. The Sentry issue was left unresolved, because nothing connects the two.\n\nThe scheduled cut at 04:15Z then failed. The reliability gate reported one high-severity issue inside its fourteen-day window, blocking short id PM-CLI-2X, reason unexpected_fault, threshold_ok false. So the daily release was blocked by a fault that no longer existed in the code being released, and the gate was correct on its own terms every step of the way: the issue was genuinely unresolved, genuinely inside the window, and genuinely classified as an unexpected fault, because the event that carried that classification was recorded before the fix and is immutable.\n\nThree facts about the recovery are worth keeping. The block cleared only when the Sentry issue was resolved by hand, not when the fix landed. The workflow already creates a Sentry release object during the release job, so resolve-in-next-release is available in the same pipeline that blocked, which is the mechanism this item proposes and it remains unused. And the window is what converts a one-off into a recurrence: an unresolved issue keeps blocking for fourteen days regardless of how long ago the code was repaired, so a single missed resolution can block up to fourteen consecutive daily cuts.\n\nThat last property explains the shape of the failure clustering measured on pm-e70zh5 the same day. Eleven of the forty most recent scheduled runs failed, and the failures arrive in clusters rather than uniformly, with four failures in the six days ending 08-10. A per-occurrence view reads each of those as an independent incident to be repaired; a windowed gate over unresolved issues predicts precisely that clustering, because one unresolved record blocks repeatedly until someone notices it is stale.\n\nThe join this item asks for is therefore not only a convenience. It is what makes the release gate's window measure current reliability rather than the age of an unclosed record."
body: ""
